test: wait for the guest to be ready instead of sleeping 2 seconds

CI's annotation finally said which scenarios failed, and the pattern was
the answer:

    FAIL [hello] [pipe] [chdir] [spawn] [sig] [sysdemo] [bad]
         [bigdir] [apps] [bigalloc]

the first eleven scenarios of the run (only `link` got lucky), while
everything from `memcheck` onwards passed.  That is not a kernel
behaviour - it is the harness typing into a shell that does not exist
yet.  qemu-test.py waited a fixed --hold (2s on CI) and then started
pressing keys; under TCG on a cold GitHub runner the first boots of a run
take much longer than that, so every keystroke went nowhere and the
scenario reported "missing" for its very first assertion.  Later runs in
the same job were fast because the images were in the page cache by then.

Fixes:

  * qemu-test.py waits for the guest's welcome banner ("Type 'help'") in
    the serial capture before typing, with --hold demoted to a ceiling
    (default 30s).  A slow machine now gets more time instead of losing
    input;
  * regress.sh passes --hold 30 by default, and CI sets TEST_HOLD=60 as
    that ceiling (it used to be "type after 2 seconds");
  * the earlier changes in this series stay: the settle floor of 20s and
    the oom case moved to a 4MB machine, which were real robustness
    problems even though they were not *the* failure.

The annotations added in 0c67f1e are what made this diagnosable at all -
the job log needs admin rights, the annotation does not.

Verified locally: fast suite 20/20 with the ready-wait in place.
